			<div class="content" id="post-content-2311071">
									

<p>I'm just not buying this one.</p>
<p>As others have said, the outcomes of the memetic effects (If not the mechanism) are pretty boring and nothing new. Put me in mind of <a href="/scp-1008">SCP-1008</a>, actually.</p>
<p>The object-which-must-must-be-perceived-or-grows was more interesting as the bird on the cereal box.</p>
<p>Ultimately, similar to sirpudding's comment, the only interesting thing about this is the prior containment and its aftereffects, and they just don't stand out for me. Particularly, why did the Foundation forget the prior containment? The anti-meme keeps people from storing perceptions of the trees, not secondary information about them, or neither the current nor the past documentation would be here.</p>
<p>Also, I love antimemetics. I loved 055, I loved the A-M department article that started the recent resurgence, and i love the extinct horizon guardians. But I feel like if I see another article cautioning against civilian mnestics in the containment procedures, my head is going to explode.</p>
<p>-1</p>

